module lib/nn_adjoints
  The backward passes (adjoints) of lib/nn's pieces, written by hand before
  `value_and_grad` could generate them. An adjoint takes the gradient of the
  loss with respect to a piece's output and returns the gradients with
  respect to its inputs and weights; running them in the reverse order of the
  forward pass is backpropagation. Nothing in a model uses them now. They are
  references: the tests hold each to the gradient `value_and_grad` generates
  from the forward pass, and the study/idioms ports that write their backward
  pass by hand call them.

linear_bwd  @bb7268cb
  what   The adjoints of a matrix product `Y = X W`, which are two more matrix
         products: `dX = dY W^T` and `dW = X^T dY`, returned in that order.
  why    Each entry of `Y` is a sum of products of a row of `X` with a column of
         `W`, so moving an entry of `X` moves a whole row of `Y` in proportion to
         a row of `W`, and the chain rule adds those contributions up as a
         product with `W^T`.
  X      the input, `T` by `D`
  W      the weights, `D` by `H`
  dY     the gradient with respect to the output, `T` by `H`

rmsnorm_bwd  @be14e34b
  what   RMSNorm's adjoint. Every entry of a row feeds the divisor, so the
         gradient of one entry depends on all of them:
         `dx = dy/r - x (dy . x) / (D r^3)`.
  X      the rows the forward pass normalized
  r      the forward pass's divisors, one per row (lib/nn's `row_rms`, raveled)
  dY     the gradient with respect to the normalized rows
  D      the number of columns
  dX     the gradient with respect to `X`
  i      a row
  dot    the row's `dy . x`
  r3     the cube of the row's divisor

relu2_bwd  @52d346c9
  what  ReLU squared's adjoint: the gradient times the derivative `2 max(x, 0)`,
        entry by entry.
  X     the forward pass's input
  dY    the gradient with respect to the output

softmax_bwd  @3cdb02b5
  what   The row softmax's adjoint, written in terms of the output rather than the
         input, which is why the forward's result is what it takes:
         `dx = y (dy - dy . y)`.
  Y      the forward pass's output
  dY     the gradient with respect to `Y`
  dX     the gradient with respect to the scores
  i      a row

attention_bwd  @d2667b7d
  what    Attention's adjoint: the gradients with respect to the rows read and the
          three projections, in that order. The mask needs no special handling
          on the way back: a masked weight is exactly zero, and softmax's adjoint
          multiplies by the weight, so the gradient there is zero without being
          told.
  how     Backwards through the forward's steps: the weighted average gives the
          gradients of the values and of the weights, softmax's adjoint turns the
          weights' gradient into the scores', and the scores give the queries' and
          keys'. Each projection then passes its share back to `X` and to its
          weight matrix.
  X       the rows the forward pass read
  Wq      the query projection
  Wk      the key projection
  Wv      the value projection
  A       the forward pass's attention weights
  dY      the gradient with respect to the head's output, `T` by `H`
  Q       the queries, computed again
  K       the keys
  V       the values
  scale   `1 / sqrt(H)`
  dV      the gradient with respect to the values: `A^T dY`
  dS      the gradient with respect to the scores
  dQ      the gradient with respect to the queries
  dK      the gradient with respect to the keys
  back    the three projections' shares of `dX`, summed

  = dV      the values' gradient: each value reached every position that attended to it
  = dS      softmax's adjoint, from the weights' gradient to the scores'
  = back    every position's row of `X` made its query, its key and its value, so its gradient has three parts

cross_entropy_bwd  @1a445eed
  what     Cross-entropy's adjoint, which is why cross-entropy and softmax are
           written together: the gradient is the predicted distribution with one
           taken off the true class, divided by the number of rows.
  why      Each row of the gradient sums to zero: raising the right class's
           probability has to lower the others'.
  logits   the scores
  target   the right class of each row
  P        the softmax of the logits, then the gradient before dividing by the
           number of rows
  i        a row
  want     the row's target

gap  @582856f2
  what  The largest gap between a hand-written gradient and the generated one,
        relative to the generated one's largest entry.
  hand       the hand-written gradient
  generated  the one `value_and_grad` generated

linear_probe  @f87cff37
  what  A probe loss for checking the matrix product's gradient with respect to
        `X`: the sum of `C * (X W)` entry by entry, for a fixed `W`.
  X     the input
  C     the fixed weights of the probe

linear_probe_w  @fcd6d496
  what  The same probe loss, as a function of `W`, for a fixed `X`.
  W     the matrix multiplied
  C     the fixed weights of the probe
